## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical observability skills and knowledge of tracing across CNPA components, a common real-world need even at beginner level.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry
- Trace propagation across HTTP, DB, and messaging
- Context extraction, sampling, and header management
- End-to-end latency verification and tooling (Jaeger, OTLP)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal OpenTelemetry setup snippet (pseudo)
const tracer = require('@opentelemetry/api').trace.getTracer('cnpa');
app.use((req,res,next)=>{ const span = tracer.startSpan('http.request', { attributes:{ route:req.path }});
  req.trace = span;
  res.on('finish', ()=> span.end());
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle sampling when traffic spikes?
- How would you verify there is no trace data loss across components?

## Why This Is Asked
In CNPA stacks, authentication rotation can create bottlenecks that affect all requests; this question evaluates your ability to diagnose cross-service latency and implement effective auth caching strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed tracing across HTTP, database, Kafka, and Redis services
- JWKS caching and cache staleness trade-offs
- Resource pooling and batch processing optimization
- Canary validation and metrics-driven verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// JWKS cache example
const jwksCache = new Map();
async function getKeys(kid){
  const cached = jwksCache.get(kid);
  if (cached && !isExpired(cached)) {
    return cached.keys;
  }
  const keys = await fetchJWKS();
  jwksCache.set(kid, { keys, timestamp: Date.now() });
  return keys;
}
```

